  Final report:
    The blog is built and running. `/` and both posts return 200, and none of them contain "Could not render component". I didn't open any page in a browser or check the layout visually.
    
    **What I built**
    - **Block types** (in the Blocks folder): `richTextBlock`, `imageBlock` and `quoteBlock`.
    - **Block grid:** a "Blog Body Block Grid" data type that allows those three blocks.
    - **Page types** (in the Pages folder): `blogFrontPage` and `blogPost`, both with the block grid as their body, each with its own template.
    - **Allowed children:** the front page is allowed under Site Root, and posts are allowed under the front page. I read each type back and the saves held.
    - **Media:** a "Blog" folder holding four Unsplash photos.
    - **Content:** a front page "Blog", published under WWW with the domain `/`. Under it are two published posts:
      - "Why MCP is awesome: one protocol, every tool"
      - "Why MCP is awesome: your CMS becomes a colleague"
    - **Post bodies:** each post has four blocks: rich text, image, quote, rich text.
    - **Razor:**
      - **Templates:** `blogFrontPage.cshtml`, `blogPost.cshtml` and a shared `_Layout.cshtml`.
      - **Block adapters:** one per block in `Views/Partials/blockgrid/Components/`.
      - **ViewComponents:** one per block (`RichText`, `Image`, `Quote`).
      - **Plain models:** one per block in `Models/Blocks/`.
      - **Rendering:** both pages call `Html.GetBlockGridHtmlAsync`.
    
    **What I checked**
    - **`get-page`:** all three pages show Published with their values.
    - **Domain:** `get-document-domains` shows `/` on the front page.
    - **Front page (`/`):** 200, one rich text block, and a listing of both posts with their images.
    - **Posts:** both return 200 with their rich text, image and quote blocks, and each has an image and a blockquote.
    - **Assets:** one post image and `/dist/site.css` both load with 200.
    - **Builds:** `dotnet build` passes with 0 errors, and `npm install` and `npm run build` ran.
    
    **How to view it**
    Umbraco is running in the background. Open https://localhost:44317/ (accept the dev certificate). The posts are at `/why-mcp-is-awesome-one-protocol-every-tool/` and `/why-mcp-is-awesome-your-cms-becomes-a-colleague/`.
    
    **Notes**
    - The first `dotnet build` hit one Razor error: `Parent<T>()` needs services passed in. I switched it to the friendly `Ancestor<BlogFrontPage>()`.
    - No MCP call failed, and I didn't work around either server.

